1
00:00:03,020 --> 00:00:05,260
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:05,520 --> 00:00:06,000
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:06,520 --> 00:00:07,520
My name is Katherine.

4
00:00:07,520 --> 00:00:13,940
And as you probably noticed on our website, this is one of many different parts of a series all about having an interview in English.

5
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:18,240
This is a job interview and at this point we're at about part 7.

6
00:00:18,240 --> 00:00:18,420
That's right.

7
00:00:19,240 --> 00:00:28,460
So today what we're going to talk about is the key question whenever you're having a job interview is they ask you to describe your key strengths.

8
00:00:28,880 --> 00:00:32,080
And this is our word for vocabulary preview today so let's take a look at it.

9
00:00:34,040 --> 00:00:35,040
Vocabulary preview.

10
00:00:35,040 --> 00:00:37,600
Okay, so you might have heard the word strength before.

11
00:00:37,600 --> 00:00:38,900
This is something that you're good at.

12
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:48,720
And so key strength or key strengths, plural, would be things that you think are most important about you as far as the things that you do well.

13
00:00:48,720 --> 00:00:48,860
That's right.

14
00:00:49,720 --> 00:00:55,400
So as we mentioned, your boss or your potential boss might ask you to describe your key strengths.

15
00:00:55,400 --> 00:01:01,640
So you're going to mention a lot of adjectives, positive adjectives saying how good of a worker you are.

16
00:01:02,260 --> 00:01:04,020
So why don't we listen to our dialogue.

17
00:01:04,400 --> 00:01:09,260
Let's see what Rebecca has to say about her key strengths and we'll look at those in a little bit.

18
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:16,580
Okay, Rebecca.

19
00:01:16,960 --> 00:01:22,000
Now you've given me a good idea of your work and academic background but what about you as a person?

20
00:01:22,540 --> 00:01:24,260
How would you describe your key strengths?

21
00:01:25,360 --> 00:01:28,970
Well Mr. Parsons, as I mentioned before, I'm someone who needs new challenges.

22
00:01:29,730 --> 00:01:31,250
I'm really focused and hardworking.

23
00:01:31,870 --> 00:01:33,710
I think my academic results prove this.

24
00:01:34,310 --> 00:01:35,370
Yes, true.

25
00:01:35,890 --> 00:01:37,770
But how about other personal qualities?

26
00:01:38,330 --> 00:01:42,030
Well I believe I'm a good team player but I can also work well independently.

27
00:01:43,110 --> 00:01:47,990
I'm very enthusiastic and well, I hope my colleagues would agree, I'm fun to work with.

28
00:01:48,650 --> 00:01:52,770
What would you say is your most positive quality?

29
00:01:54,190 --> 00:01:57,890
That's a tough question but I would have to say my passion.

30
00:01:58,510 --> 00:02:02,230
I'm really passionate about journalism and passionate about my career.

31
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:09,960
All right so she obviously has a lot of good key strengths.

32
00:02:09,970 --> 00:02:12,470
Why don't we analyze a couple of those on Language Takeaway.

33
00:02:14,900 --> 00:02:15,800
Language Takeaway.

34
00:02:17,130 --> 00:02:20,870
So one of the first things that Rebecca mentions is that she's really focused.

35
00:02:21,510 --> 00:02:24,090
Okay, so this word focused, what does this mean?

36
00:02:24,520 --> 00:02:31,800
All right, so if a person is focused, basically you're saying that you concentrate on your work.

37
00:02:31,800 --> 00:02:44,850
That's right and someone who's focused might be very good in a noisy office or in a place that is full of distractions like you said because they don't stop their work and think about the noise or move around or go talk to people.

38
00:02:45,600 --> 00:02:48,550
Someone who is focused just sits down and can do a lot of work all at once.

39
00:02:49,120 --> 00:02:54,360
That's right, so she describes herself as focused and hardworking.

40
00:02:54,360 --> 00:02:58,370
So hardworking means basically what it sounds like, that someone works very hard.

41
00:02:58,680 --> 00:03:05,840
That means they put in a lot of hours, they get their work done, they're really, really dependable.

42
00:03:06,230 --> 00:03:10,690
That's right, so as you can see we use the word hardworking together.

43
00:03:11,030 --> 00:03:15,590
So if you work hard, you don't say I work hard.

44
00:03:15,790 --> 00:03:20,610
You can say that but if you want to use it as an adjective, you say I am hardworking.

45
00:03:21,310 --> 00:03:22,830
The opposite of course would be lazy.

46
00:03:23,410 --> 00:03:23,910
That's right.

47
00:03:23,910 --> 00:03:31,810
Okay, so we're focused, we're hardworking and she also said that she is a good team player.

48
00:03:32,430 --> 00:03:42,310
Okay, this is a really key phrase here because it's something that employers, so people who are hiring new staff like to look out for because most jobs you don't work alone.

49
00:03:42,550 --> 00:03:48,930
You work with other people and so a team player is someone who works well in a group or works well with others.

50
00:03:49,370 --> 00:03:56,110
That's right, so a team player, everyone wants to have a good team player working for their team, for their company.

51
00:03:56,690 --> 00:04:00,230
And apart from being a good team player, you also want to be enthusiastic.

52
00:04:00,970 --> 00:04:06,390
That's right, so this means that you are interested in what you do, you are excited about it.

53
00:04:06,670 --> 00:04:09,270
It's not like oh God, I have to do this report.

54
00:04:09,450 --> 00:04:14,790
It's like oh wow, I get to do this report, I'm enthusiastic, I'm really interested, I'm going to put a lot of energy into it.

55
00:04:14,990 --> 00:04:15,510
That's right.

56
00:04:16,110 --> 00:04:25,430
So being an enthusiastic person not only can be about working, you can be enthusiastic about anything, about painting your house, about going on a trip.

57
00:04:25,870 --> 00:04:27,050
Exactly, enthusiastic.

58
00:04:28,170 --> 00:04:35,470
Alright and our last word for language, takeaway is also a good quality to have if you are passionate about what you do or what you're doing.

59
00:04:35,940 --> 00:04:39,530
So someone who is passionate about something really, really likes that thing.

60
00:04:39,760 --> 00:04:42,970
That means that they love to do it or love to see it.

61
00:04:43,670 --> 00:04:46,290
Passion is the opposite of not interested.

62
00:04:47,250 --> 00:04:50,790
So you're really, really emotional and excited and interested in something.

63
00:04:51,270 --> 00:04:53,770
That's right, so you are passionate about something.

64
00:04:54,560 --> 00:05:02,320
It comes from the noun passion, so you have a passion for painting, maybe you love to paint.

65
00:05:02,320 --> 00:05:06,040
So you're very passionate about painting.

66
00:05:06,040 --> 00:05:10,480
So a lot of great adjectives there to describe yourself in a good way.

67
00:05:10,480 --> 00:05:17,960
Why don't we listen to our dialogue again and we'll be back with Fluency Builder.

68
00:05:17,960 --> 00:05:24,200
Okay Rebecca, now you've given me a good idea of your work and academic background, but what about you as a person?

69
00:05:24,200 --> 00:05:26,000
How would you describe your key strengths?

70
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:30,610
Well Mr. Parsons, as I mentioned before, I'm someone who needs new challenges.

71
00:05:31,330 --> 00:05:32,890
I'm really focused and hardworking.

72
00:05:33,490 --> 00:05:35,370
I think my academic results prove this.

73
00:05:35,910 --> 00:05:37,010
Yes, true.

74
00:05:37,530 --> 00:05:39,410
But how about other personal qualities?

75
00:05:39,970 --> 00:05:43,690
Well I believe I'm a good team player, but I can also work well independently.

76
00:05:44,730 --> 00:05:49,630
I'm very enthusiastic and well, I hope my colleagues would agree, I'm fun to work with.

77
00:05:50,370 --> 00:05:54,310
What would you say is your most positive quality?

78
00:05:55,120 --> 00:05:57,960
Hmm, that's a tough question.

79
00:05:57,960 --> 00:05:59,560
But I would have to say my passion.

80
00:06:00,160 --> 00:06:03,860
I'm really passionate about journalism and passionate about my career.

81
00:06:08,560 --> 00:06:12,760
Alright so on Fluency Builder today we've picked out three key phrases.

82
00:06:12,760 --> 00:06:13,860
Let's take a look at the first one.

83
00:06:14,280 --> 00:06:18,780
When Rebecca said, I'm someone who needs new challenges.

84
00:06:19,920 --> 00:06:27,800
Okay so this structure, I'm someone who, is something that you hear a lot of and you can actually fill in the end of that sentence with a number of different phrases.

85
00:06:28,120 --> 00:06:31,400
So in here we heard, I'm someone who needs new challenges.

86
00:06:31,920 --> 00:06:34,900
We could also say, I'm someone who needs structure.

87
00:06:35,500 --> 00:06:41,060
Alright so we can also change the verb right from needs to, I'm someone who likes a challenge.

88
00:06:42,140 --> 00:06:47,640
Or maybe Tracy is someone who requires a lot of attention.

89
00:06:48,200 --> 00:06:52,560
Right, so this is a way to introduce a characteristic of yourself.

90
00:06:53,440 --> 00:06:55,180
So you're telling people something about yourself.

91
00:06:55,640 --> 00:06:56,600
Okay, very good.

92
00:06:57,140 --> 00:07:02,800
So moving on, we also saw that Rebecca said that she is fun to work with.

93
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:04,120
I'm fun to work with.

94
00:07:04,510 --> 00:07:06,810
Okay so let's just treat this like a little fixed phrase.

95
00:07:07,510 --> 00:07:09,330
We say this about people all the time.

96
00:07:09,570 --> 00:07:14,010
He's fun to work with, she's fun to work with, or this group is really fun to work with.

97
00:07:14,190 --> 00:07:15,510
They're all really fun to work with.

98
00:07:15,670 --> 00:07:19,190
So that means that it's not serious, that you really enjoy doing it.

99
00:07:19,390 --> 00:07:29,010
So it's a little fixed phrase that you can use to say that it's nice to work with Rebecca or that she's very, not serious, not a mean person.

100
00:07:29,410 --> 00:07:29,910
That's right.

101
00:07:30,720 --> 00:07:38,150
And finally we have a phrase at the end, Rebecca hears a question she thinks is very difficult, she calls it a tough question.

102
00:07:39,080 --> 00:07:42,920
And so a tough question is something that is very hard to answer.

103
00:07:42,920 --> 00:07:45,130
That's right, she says, hmm, that's a tough question.

104
00:07:45,670 --> 00:07:50,490
So she's basically acknowledging the fact that the question is difficult, a tough question.

105
00:07:51,030 --> 00:07:53,110
Now we can combine this word tough with other things.

106
00:07:53,500 --> 00:07:57,640
That's right, you can say it's a tough situation, that means it's a hard situation to handle.

107
00:07:58,240 --> 00:08:03,880
You could also say he's having a tough time, that means that right now in his life there are many things that are difficult for him.

108
00:08:04,040 --> 00:08:06,960
And so tough means difficult or hard or unpleasant.

109
00:08:07,300 --> 00:08:08,360
Okay, very good.

110
00:08:08,620 --> 00:08:13,400
So a lot of good stuff coming from Rebecca, mentioning all the positive things about her.

111
00:08:13,800 --> 00:08:15,740
Why don't we listen to her dialogue one last time.

112
00:08:22,830 --> 00:08:28,910
Okay Rebecca, now you've given me a good idea of your work and academic background, but what about you as a person?

113
00:08:29,480 --> 00:08:31,230
How would you describe your key strengths?

114
00:08:32,280 --> 00:08:35,970
Well Mr. Parsons, as I mentioned before, I'm someone who needs new challenges.

115
00:08:36,630 --> 00:08:38,170
I'm really focused and hardworking.

116
00:08:38,810 --> 00:08:40,610
I think my academic results prove this.

117
00:08:41,130 --> 00:08:42,310
Yes, true.

118
00:08:42,670 --> 00:08:44,730
But how about other personal qualities?

119
00:08:45,230 --> 00:08:48,990
Well I believe I'm a good team player, but I can also work well independently.

120
00:08:49,930 --> 00:08:55,390
I'm very enthusiastic and, well, I hope my colleagues would agree, I'm fun to work with.

121
00:08:55,700 --> 00:08:59,680
What would you say is your most positive quality?

122
00:09:00,160 --> 00:09:02,160
Hmm, that's a tough question.

123
00:09:03,240 --> 00:09:04,780
But I would have to say my passion.

124
00:09:05,400 --> 00:09:09,040
I'm really passionate about journalism and passionate about my career.

125
00:09:15,850 --> 00:09:26,680
Alright so talking about key strengths, I think it's always difficult to talk about yourself and still sound a little bit humble, right, to not sound arrogant like saying I'm the best.

126
00:09:27,200 --> 00:09:28,720
Right, well that's the key here I think.

127
00:09:28,940 --> 00:09:33,320
You want to say really good things about yourself because you're a salesperson and you're selling your personality.

128
00:09:33,780 --> 00:09:35,640
But at the same time you don't want to sound like a jerk.

129
00:09:36,360 --> 00:09:38,060
Like I'm the best, I'm so smart, I'm so clever.

130
00:09:38,960 --> 00:09:41,620
That's a little bit off-putting, it makes people uncomfortable.

131
00:09:42,120 --> 00:09:43,340
So I think the trick is a balance.

132
00:09:44,420 --> 00:09:52,580
And I guess if you use words such as this, like you know, I'm focused, I'm hardworking, I'm independent, I have a passion for this.

133
00:09:53,000 --> 00:10:01,480
It makes you sound like you're a really good worker but you're not arrogant, you're not saying oh well, you know, I'm the best of what I do or something like that.

134
00:10:01,880 --> 00:10:08,260
Right, so you're basically saying these are my skills but obviously I'm not the only person who has these great skills.

135
00:10:08,800 --> 00:10:08,960
That's right.

136
00:10:09,800 --> 00:10:11,100
Okay so that's all the time we have for today.

137
00:10:11,360 --> 00:10:13,960
Be sure to join us next week.

138
00:10:14,080 --> 00:10:17,900
We're also going to have kind of part two of this when we talk about our weaknesses.

139
00:10:18,280 --> 00:10:22,240
So this time we talked about our strengths, then we'll talk about weaknesses.

140
00:10:22,240 --> 00:10:23,240
That's right.

141
00:10:23,240 --> 00:10:25,440
So we hope that you will check out our website EnglishPod.com.

142
00:10:25,440 --> 00:10:28,860
If you have questions or comments, leave them there for us and we'll be in touch very soon.

143
00:10:29,040 --> 00:10:29,840
All right, we'll see you guys there.

144
00:10:29,880 --> 00:10:30,880
Bye.

145
